<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Company of Merchants Trading to Africa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1752–1821</span> · Also known as African Company of Merchants</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Company of Merchants Trading to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was a British association, created by act of Parliament in 1750, that took over the West African forts of the Royal African Company in 1752. Funded by a parliamentary grant, it maintained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast-castle">Cape Coast Castle</a> and other posts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> for all British traders, chiefly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. It was dissolved in 1821, when the crown placed its forts under the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>.</p></div>
